{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The lotteries have distinct outcomes and probabilities. Lottery A offers 2400 Euros with 100% probability. Lottery B offers 2500 Euros with 33% probability, 2400 Euros with 66% probability, and 0 Euros with 1% probability. The favorable outcome can be interpreted as receiving a non-zero amount (or at least 2400 Euros, given the context). If differences between outcomes (e.g., 2400 vs. 2500) are considered negligible, then the key comparison becomes the probability of achieving a favorable outcome. Lottery A has a 100% chance of yielding 2400 Euros. Lottery B has a 99% chance of yielding either 2500 or 2400 (favorable), and a 1% chance of 0. Thus, Lottery A has a higher probability (100%) of a favorable outcome than Lottery B (99%), assuming 0 is unfavorable and all positive outcomes are favorable. Therefore, the reason would prefer Lottery A.",
